# Markdown documents

Generate documentation only when requested or required by an applicable instruction. Edit the existing source of truth instead of creating parallel guides.

- Keep user instructions short, actionable and consistent with the actual setup/start commands. AI tooling stays optional.
- Preserve the document's established language, structure and useful metadata. Update an existing date, version, reproducing statement or changelog when the change warrants it; do not add a metadata ceremony to every template or small guide.
- Use plain language, descriptive links, inline code for paths and commands, and language-tagged fences for multiline examples.
- Provide meaningful alt text for documentation images.
- Verify affected local links and anchors. Clearly distinguish historical evidence from current behavior and actual results from unverified claims.
- Do not create directory READMEs, duplicate architecture guidance or request review checkpoints solely to satisfy a formatting convention.
